{"about":"67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e","body":"The conjecture commits a straw man fallacy by suggesting Popper advises preference based on 'past performance' as a guide to 'future reliability.' Popper argues that we have no choice but to act, and in the absence of certainty, the only non-arbitrary criterion is the degree of corroboration. This is not smuggling in induction but acknowledging the necessity of decision-making under uncertainty while rigorously avoiding the claim that the decision guarantees truth or probability.\n[would settle: An examination of Popper's specific responses to critics who accused him of cryptic inductivism, particularly his later works where he refines the concept of verisimilitude.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"TARGET CONJECTURE 67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e"}